1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,015
Hi again. In this video,

2
00:00:03,015 --> 00:00:05,145
we're going to learn
about a common strategy

3
00:00:05,145 --> 00:00:07,965
used to resolve
problems in a project.

4
00:00:07,965 --> 00:00:10,035
On top of several other tasks,

5
00:00:10,035 --> 00:00:12,180
it's a project manager's
responsibility

6
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:13,335
to resolve problems

7
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:14,790
and remove constraints that are

8
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:17,310
a detriment to the
project's success.

9
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:20,265
One way to do this
is to escalate.

10
00:00:20,265 --> 00:00:23,790
Escalation is the process
of enlisting the help of

11
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:25,740
higher-level project
leadership or

12
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:28,050
management to remove an obstacle,

13
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:30,285
clarify or reinforce priorities,

14
00:00:30,285 --> 00:00:32,335
and validate next steps.

15
00:00:32,335 --> 00:00:35,805
Escalation may seem to have
a negative connotation,

16
00:00:35,805 --> 00:00:38,025
but that's not the case
in project management.

17
00:00:38,025 --> 00:00:41,030
In project management,
escalation should be encouraged,

18
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:43,489
used often, and even celebrated.

19
00:00:43,489 --> 00:00:46,610
Escalations are healthy
and act as a checks and

20
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:47,810
balances tool to make

21
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:49,910
sure appropriate
action is being taken.

22
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:53,135
They can result in speedy
decision-making, and generally,

23
00:00:53,135 --> 00:00:55,025
the quicker a
problem is resolved,

24
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:56,920
the better off a project will be.

25
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:58,790
Instead of going back and forth

26
00:00:58,790 --> 00:01:01,115
between two team members
who can't agree,

27
00:01:01,115 --> 00:01:03,410
an objective third
party might help make

28
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:06,280
the call and reduce
frustrations within the team.

29
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:09,435
Finally, escalations
encourage participation.

30
00:01:09,435 --> 00:01:12,650
Encouraging others to
participate in solving or owning

31
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:14,390
problems can promote trust and

32
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:16,580
shared responsibility
among teammates,

33
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:19,540
which are signs of a
healthy, functional team.

34
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:22,850
Before starting work on a
project, the project manager,

35
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:24,950
the team, and the
project sponsor should

36
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:27,970
establish escalation
standards and practices.

37
00:01:27,970 --> 00:01:29,525
This means they'll specify

38
00:01:29,525 --> 00:01:31,325
who the issues will be raised to,

39
00:01:31,325 --> 00:01:32,795
how issues are raised,

40
00:01:32,795 --> 00:01:34,744
and the forum for discussion.

41
00:01:34,744 --> 00:01:37,370
A little upfront work
will help escalations run

42
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:40,360
smoothly when and
if they're needed.

43
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:43,220
So you know what an
escalation is and when to

44
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:45,890
set the framework for
managing escalations,

45
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:48,245
but how do you know
when to escalate?

46
00:01:48,245 --> 00:01:50,840
A project manager should
escalate an issue at

47
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:53,570
the first sign of critical
problems in the project.

48
00:01:53,570 --> 00:01:56,060
Critical problems are
issues that may cause

49
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:58,580
a delay to a major
project milestone,

50
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,515
issues that cause
budget overruns,

51
00:02:00,515 --> 00:02:03,050
issues that can result in
the loss of a customer,

52
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:04,520
and issues that push back

53
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:06,625
the estimated project
completion date.

54
00:02:06,625 --> 00:02:08,150
Basically, anything that will

55
00:02:08,150 --> 00:02:09,635
affect your triple constraint,

56
00:02:09,635 --> 00:02:11,645
time, budget, and scope

57
00:02:11,645 --> 00:02:13,580
is something that
should be escalated.

58
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,230
Escalation is great for

59
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:18,215
preventing two common
issues within a project:

60
00:02:18,215 --> 00:02:20,935
trench wars and bad compromises.

61
00:02:20,935 --> 00:02:24,530
Trench wars occur when
two peers or groups can't

62
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:26,120
seem to come to an agreement, and

63
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:27,980
neither party is
willing to give in.

64
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:30,650
This leads to a standstill
of the project and will

65
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:34,150
likely delay certain aspects
of the project's progress.

66
00:02:34,150 --> 00:02:36,410
Typically, we think
of compromises

67
00:02:36,410 --> 00:02:38,995
as a positive way
to resolve issues,

68
00:02:38,995 --> 00:02:42,290
but there is such a thing
as a bad compromise.

69
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:44,270
A bad compromise occurs when

70
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:47,225
two parties settle on
a so-called solution,

71
00:02:47,225 --> 00:02:49,450
but the end product
still suffers.

72
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:53,100
When it comes to compromising
on important project goals,

73
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:55,250
it's not productive
for either party to

74
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:57,920
settle simply because
it's a means to an end.

75
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:00,980
Yes, compromise
while also keeping

76
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:02,675
the larger project goals in mind

77
00:03:02,675 --> 00:03:04,775
and working towards
those together.

78
00:03:04,775 --> 00:03:06,880
You may have to help
your team or stakeholders

79
00:03:06,881 --> 00:03:10,120
make hard choices
for the greater good.

80
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:12,530
So now we've defined escalation,

81
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:14,840
how to use the triple
constraints model when

82
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:17,915
communicating to stakeholders
and negotiating changes,

83
00:03:17,915 --> 00:03:20,930
and we learned about trench
wars and bad compromises,

84
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:23,305
which are two common
issues on a project.

85
00:03:23,305 --> 00:03:24,650
In the next video,

86
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:26,450
we'll explain some
more techniques

87
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:27,995
for communicating with your team,

88
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:30,380
including timeouts
and retrospectives.

89
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:32,340
I'll meet you there.
